{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>ze ha'isheh</b> — three tagin on the last heh of ha'isheh, to tell you: five types of offerings I have given you (ḥatat, olah, asham, shelamim, todah). And the olah is the most beloved of all, and its treatment spans five fifths of the Torah, and I account it to you as if you offered them — five types of offerings." "<b>temimim shnayim layom</b> — rashei tevot תש״ל, to tell you that the gematria of תש״ל is the number of tamid offerings brought in a year." "<b>layom olah tamid</b> — read it olah (with segol), to tell you that it is slaughtered in the place of the day's olah. Every olah written in the festivals is defective except the olah of Atzeret, which is plene — a hint that on the sixth of Sivan the Torah was given. In all the festivals ḥag is stated except Shavuot, because sometimes it is postponed if it falls on Shabbat (according to Beit Shammai) — a day of slaughter for the next day — but on Pesach and Sukkot one may bring during the intermediate days of the festival, whereas Shavuot's make-up is not on the festival. In all of them lekhattat is stated except Atzeret, because the Torah was given on it, which atones for those who learn it."], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {2 "The phrase 'יום טבוח למחר' appears to be a shorthand reference to a tannaitic dispute (Beit Shammai) concerning whether Shavuot can fall the day after Shabbat; translated hyper-literally as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:23:28.206388346Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}